{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1594776539/5/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e4941b18\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nMove red into teal's top marker position, then move teal to the bottom outside corner of the grey rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant background surrounds one filled rectangular non-background block.\n- There is exactly one red marker and one teal marker, both in the row immediately above the rectangle.\n- Both markers are horizontally aligned over columns inside the rectangle.\n\nRule\n- Clear the original red marker.\n- Recolor the original teal marker cell red.\n- Place the teal marker beside the rectangle's bottom row, on the side indicated by the horizontal direction from red to teal.\n\nKey insight\n- The red-to-teal horizontal order chooses the rectangle side where teal exits at the bottom.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"1818057f\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nDetect thin '+' shapes in a single foreground color and recolor them to teal.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- One foreground color (e.g. yellow) forms various shapes on the bg, some of which are small '+' crosses.\n\nRule:\n- A '+' center is any foreground pixel whose 4 orthogonal neighbors are also foreground.\n- Recolor each such center and its 4 neighbors to teal (8).\n- All other pixels remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight: Only compact 5-cell crosses qualify — a pixel embedded in a larger filled region also satisfies the neighbor check, so the rule highlights every local '+' motif, not just isolated ones.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8b9c3697\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nInsert red components into interior sockets of large structures, leaving a black trail.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common. Large non-bg, non-red structures with interior cavities.\n- Red components outside structures.\n\nRule:\n- Interior sockets: bg cells inside a structure's bbox, adjacent to structure pixels.\n- For each structure, find the direction where rays from sockets hit a red component.\n- Slide that component into the socket; paint traversed cells black.\n- Unmatched reds vanish.\n\nKey insight: Docking direction is inferred by ray-casting from interior sockets to the nearest red component.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ad3b40cf\" lines=\"72-87\">\n<description>\nMirror the smallest non-axis foreground color across the full straight axis.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One foreground color forms exactly one full row, column, main diagonal, or anti-diagonal reflection axis.\n- The remaining foreground colors include a unique least-populated color to copy.\n\nRule\n- Preserve the input grid.\n- Select every pixel of the unique smallest non-axis foreground color.\n- Reflect those pixels across the full straight axis and paint the reflected positions with the same color.\n\nKey insight\n- The axis color marks the mirror line, while the color population chooses which set of objects is duplicated.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1190bc91\" lines=\"36-49\">\n<description>\nComplete a sparse grid by casting occluding diagonal rays from the longest run and filling the remaining diagonal-connected regions from the short bars.\n\nInput structure\n- Black-background grid with one unique longest 4-connected foreground object.\n- The longest object is a horizontal or vertical multicolor run; all other foreground objects are shorter monochrome bars.\n\nRule\n- On a blank canvas, process the longest run from left to right or top to bottom; each pixel emits four 45-degree rays that stop at the first already-painted pixel.\n- Each remaining monochrome bar fills every still-blank pixel reachable from it by diagonal steps.\n\nKey insight\n- Earlier pixels in the long run occlude later diagonal rays, and those ray barriers partition the background into diagonal-connected regions for the short bars.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"36a08778\" lines=\"8-21\">\n<description>\nSimulate water falling from top-edge sources, pooling on shelves, and cascading off edges.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; water = rarest non-bg; shelf = other non-bg.\n- Sources: water clusters touching top row. Shelves: horizontal segments.\n\nRule:\n- Water falls down from sources, painting bg cells as water.\n- On hitting a shelf, it spreads across the shelf span plus one cell past each end.\n- From those overflow positions, water resumes falling independently.\n\nKey insight: Each shelf splits a stream into two at its edges, creating a branching waterfall tree.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1be83260\" lines=\"41-58\">\n<description>\nDecode the colored slot map inside the marked motif and rebuild the motif lattice with those colors.\n\nInput structure:\n- A blank background surrounds a rectangular lattice of identical motifs.\n- The motif rows and columns are separated by one background line.\n- One motif has colored marker pixels in its internal odd-row/odd-column slots.\n\nRule:\n- The motif size is 2R+1 by 2C+1, where R by C is the motif lattice size.\n- Read the marked motif's odd internal slots as the color map for the R by C output motifs.\n- Slots that still contain the repeated motif color map to that repeated motif color.\n- Rebuild the compact lattice on a canvas filled with the repeated motif color,\n  painting each motif mask with its decoded color.\n\nKey insight:\n- The holes and filled odd slots inside one motif are a miniature legend for recoloring the entire repeated motif lattice.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"20270e3b\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nConnect two sprites using matching connector pixels, then tight-crop.\n\nInput structure:\n- Background color `bg`, sprite color `obj`, connector color `ctrl` (small, 2 components).\n- Two main foreground sprites, plus possibly tiny detached pixels belonging to one sprite.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the **base** sprite (closest to grid top-left).\n- Translate the other sprite so its connector sits one cell above the base's connector.\n- Stamp the plug onto the base; remove connectors (recolor `ctrl` to `obj`).\n- Output is the tight crop around all non-background pixels.\n\nKey insight: The connector pixels define the unique docking point and direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f669b64\" lines=\"26-48\">\n<description>\nPush the central solid rectangle to the grid edge, splitting the perpendicular target rectangle around its path.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly three monochrome foreground objects arranged on one\n  horizontal or vertical axis.\n- The middle object on that axis is a solid rectangular moving object.\n- One end object is a solid rectangle or bar whose long side is perpendicular\n  to the movement axis; this is the target to split.\n\nRule\n- Move the middle rectangle along the axis toward the target until it is\n  flush with the grid edge in that direction.\n- Clear the original middle rectangle and the original target.\n- Split the target through the moving rectangle's two-cell-wide path and\n  shift the two halves one cell outward perpendicular to the movement.\n- Keep the other end object unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The non-target end object acts as a pusher; the central rectangle travels\n  away from it through the perpendicular target, opening a matching gap.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6cbe9eb8\" lines=\"79-94\">\n<description>\nRebuild the foreground rectangles as a largest-to-smallest lower-left nesting.\n\nInput structure\n- A periodic wallpaper background is overlaid by foreground colors that each form a rectangular frame or solid rectangle.\n- The largest foreground rectangle is the output frame; smaller foreground rectangles are the objects to nest inside it.\n- A frame may be partially hidden by a smaller rectangle, but its visible pixels still determine its bbox.\n\nRule\n- Ignore the wallpaper and crop the result to the largest foreground rectangle's bbox size.\n- Sort foreground rectangles by bbox area from largest to smallest.\n- Reconstruct each frame or solid rectangle, placing every next smaller rectangle in the lower-left of the current rectangle's usable region.\n\nKey insight\n- The scattered background colors are not objects; the meaningful order is the unique descending rectangle size chain, and each step nests into the previous rectangle's lower-left interior.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"21897d95\" lines=\"283-301\">\n<description>\nRepaint regions from T-shaped control markers, erase the markers, and optionally rotate based on a color-9 L-marker.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a mosaic of monochrome regions.\n- Color 1 forms T-shaped control markers on region boundaries.\n- Some controls use three singleton color-1 pixels around a colored center; that center is a literal replacement color.\n- An optional 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker triggers 90-degree rotation of the output.\n\nRule:\n- For a color-1 T marker, the single stem points at the destination region to repaint.\n- The two diagonal cells behind the stem identify the source region; the destination gets that source region's original color.\n- For a colored-center T marker, the same stem identifies the destination, but the center color is the replacement.\n- All marker cells are filled with the final color of the source-side region.\n- A 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker controls rotation: ┐ (corner top-right) → CCW 90°, ┌ (corner top-left) → CW 90°.\n\nKey insight:\n- The stem chooses what changes, while the two diagonals behind the stem choose where the marker itself belongs.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"faa9f03d\" lines=\"36-52\">\n<description>\nRepair a one-cell-thick colored line network by replacing singleton markers, filling short orthogonal gaps, and redirecting yellow-marked arms to the edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- A rectangular grid with a dominant background.\n- Line colors form one-cell-thick horizontal/vertical stroke fragments.\n- Marker colors are exactly those whose 4-connected components are all singleton pixels.\n- Red singleton markers are missing line cells; yellow singleton markers are directional controls next to a line endpoint.\n\nRule:\n- Assign every marker to the nearest visible line color in row/column line-of-sight, preferring an endpoint supported along that same axis.\n- For each line color, fill the gap between consecutive facing endpoints in a row or column.\n- A yellow control erases the old straight arm on the far side of its endpoint and paints the same color from that endpoint through the control pixel to the grid edge.\n- At crossings, yellow-control rays win; otherwise an original-vs-filled crossing uses the more fragmented pre-repair line color, while pure filled crossings use the lower color id.\n\nKey insight: The singleton markers identify which sparse stroke fragments belong together; after they are colored, the intended lines are the axis-aligned gaps between facing endpoints, with yellow controls flipping one arm to the marked side.\n</description>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
