{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1594776539/5/codegen_prompts/7.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1594776539/5`\n- 题号：`7`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1594776539/5/7.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"7\">\n- `description`：网格中多个同色对象共享一条隐含的对称轴，找出每组对象的对称轴后，将轴线另一侧缺失的像素补全，使每组对象关于该轴完全对称。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为 black。\n  - 网格中有若干组彩色像素对象，每组由同一种颜色的若干个分散像素块（不一定连通）构成，不同组颜色不同（如 red、blue、green 等）。\n  - 每组同色像素在设计上几乎关于某条水平线或垂直线对称，但有若干像素被故意删除，导致对称性被破坏。\n  - 每组对象的对称轴不在网格上显式标注，需要解题者推断。对称轴位于某两行之间或某两列之间（即轴在半像素位置上，不占据实际像素行/列）。\n  - 每种颜色的对称轴方向和位置可能不同。\n  - 网格中还有一组 grey 像素形成一条显式的直线（水平或垂直），该直线不是任何一组彩色对象的对称轴，而是一条\"全局分割线\"，将网格分为两个半区。每组彩色对象完全位于其中一个半区内（不跨越 grey 线）。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 忽略 grey 分割线，它仅用于视觉分隔，不参与变换。\n  - 对每组同色像素，找到使该组像素最接近完全对称的那条轴线（水平或垂直，位于两行之间或两列之间的半像素位置）。具体地，遍历所有可能的半像素轴位置，选择使反射后重合像素数最多的那条轴。\n  - 将该组像素中每个像素关于其对称轴反射，如果反射位置是 black 背景，则在该位置绘制同色像素。\n  - 原有像素保持不变。grey 线保持不变。\n\n- `Key insight`：解题者需要发现每组同色对象拥有各自独立的隐含对称轴，且轴的方向和位置各不相同。关键推理在于：（1）从不完全对称的像素分布中推断出最佳对称轴位置；（2）理解对称轴是半像素级别的（位于两行/列之间而非某行/列上），这使得反射后的像素不会与原像素重叠。grey 分割线是干扰项，测试解题者能否区分视觉装饰与功能性结构。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：train/ad3b40cf, train/9f669b64, train/34cfa167, train/f0f8a26d, train/3bd292e8, eval/b10624e5, train/6350f1f4, train/a09f6c25\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ad3b40cf\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3927\nmechanism=0.5038\nlexical=0.0104\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:axis, object:sprite, operation:reflect, output:same_size, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/ad3b40cf.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/ad3b40cf.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nMirror the smallest non-axis foreground color across the full straight axis.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One foreground color forms exactly one full row, column, main diagonal, or anti-diagonal reflection axis.\n- The remaining foreground colors include a unique least-populated color to copy.\n\nRule\n- Preserve the input grid.\n- Select every pixel of the unique smallest non-axis foreground color.\n- Reflect those pixels across the full straight axis and paint the reflected positions with the same color.\n\nKey insight\n- The axis color marks the mirror line, while the color population chooses which set of objects is duplicated.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"foreground scene with one full straight reflection axis and unequal-area non-axis color masks\",\n  \"dst\": \"same canvas with the least-populated non-axis color mask completed across the axis\",\n  \"transform\": \"mirror every pixel of the unique least-populated non-axis color across the row, column, main-diagonal, or anti-diagonal axis; leave all original pixels unchanged\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f669b64\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3743\nmechanism=0.4417\nlexical=0.0099\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:axis, object:line_segment, object:sprite, output:same_size, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/9f669b64.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/9f669b64.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nPush the central solid rectangle to the grid edge, splitting the perpendicular target rectangle around its path.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly three monochrome foreground objects arranged on one\n  horizontal or vertical axis.\n- The middle object on that axis is a solid rectangular moving object.\n- One end object is a solid rectangle or bar whose long side is perpendicular\n  to the movement axis; this is the target to split.\n\nRule\n- Move the middle rectangle along the axis toward the target until it is\n  flush with the grid edge in that direction.\n- Clear the original middle rectangle and the original target.\n- Split the target through the moving rectangle's two-cell-wide path and\n  shift the two halves one cell outward perpendicular to the movement.\n- Keep the other end object unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The non-target end object acts as a pusher; the central rectangle travels\n  away from it through the perpendicular target, opening a matching gap.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"middle solid rectangular object between two end objects\",\n  \"path\": \"straight horizontal or vertical line away from the non-target end object and through the perpendicular solid target rectangle/bar\",\n  \"dst\": \"place flush with the grid edge beyond the target\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"paint only the final moved rectangle at dst\",\n  \"transform\": \"erase original source pixels; split the target rectangle/bar along the moving object's two-cell-wide path and shift its two halves one cell outward perpendicular to the movement; keep the other end object unchanged\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"34cfa167\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3345\nmechanism=0.4184\nlexical=0.0101\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:line_segment, operation:reflect, output:same_size, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/34cfa167.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/34cfa167.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nComplete a four-corner framed rectangle from one decorated anchor rectangle and its diagonal mate.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Two same-color solid rectangular anchors of the same size sit on opposite\n  diagonal corners, with the first anchor above-left of the second.\n- The upper-left anchor has two one-cell-thick vertical control bars to its\n  right and two one-cell-thick horizontal control bars below it.\n\nRule:\n- Copy the anchor rectangle into the two missing corners of the implied\n  larger rectangle.\n- Repeat the right-side control bars horizontally between the left and right\n  anchors, using their spacing as the period, and draw the outer top and\n  bottom borders in the first right-control color.\n- Repeat the below-side control bars vertically between the top and bottom\n  anchors, using the same period, and draw the outer left and right borders\n  in the first below-control color.\n\nKey insight: The small control bars are the first two ticks of two periodic\nframe patterns; the diagonal anchor supplies the opposite corner and the\nmissing two anchor copies.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"two same-color solid rectangular anchors, with two right control bars and two below control bars beside the upper-left anchor\",\n  \"dst\": \"the missing two anchor corners and periodic frame segments between all four anchors\",\n  \"transform\": \"copy the anchor rectangle into the missing corners; repeat the right-control colors across the top and bottom anchor rows and outer borders, and repeat the below-control colors down the left and right side bars using the same spacing\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f0f8a26d\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3150\nmechanism=0.3543\nlexical=0.0104\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:line_segment, object:sprite, operation:reflect, output:same_size, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/f0f8a26d.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/f0f8a26d.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRotate every foreground bar 90 degrees around its own center on the same canvas.\n\nInput structure\nThe background is the dominant color, and all foreground pixels share one color.\nEach 4-connected foreground object is a solid one-cell-thick horizontal or vertical\nbar with odd length, with single-pixel bars allowed.\n\nRule\nClear the canvas to the background, then redraw every bar with its midpoint, length,\nand color preserved while swapping horizontal and vertical orientation.\n\nKey insight\nThe bars are independent objects: their centers and lengths identify the output\nplacement, so no matching, ordering, or global symmetry is needed.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"4-connected monochromatic foreground bars on a dominant background\",\n  \"transform\": \"rotate each odd-length horizontal or vertical bar 90 degrees about its midpoint; preserve its color and length, with single-pixel bars unchanged\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3bd292e8\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3091\nmechanism=0.3614\nlexical=0.0167\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:line_segment, operation:reflect, output:same_size, relation:between, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/3bd292e8.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/3bd292e8.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRecolor wall-separated background regions alternately, starting with grey in the lower-left region.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one dominant background color and one foreground wall color.\n- The wall pixels form one or more 4-connected one-cell-thick segments.\n- Each wall segment separates exactly two 4-connected background regions.\nRule\n- Keep all wall pixels unchanged.\n- Label the 4-connected background regions and connect two labels when they\n  touch opposite sides of the same wall segment.\n- Color the region containing the lower-left corner grey, then flip between\n  grey and green across every wall adjacency.\nKey insight\n- The red walls define a bipartite map of regions; the lower-left background\n  region fixes which side of the alternating coloring is grey.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"4-connected orange background regions separated by red one-cell-thick wall segments\",\n  \"transform\": \"keep red wall pixels; recolor the lower-left background region grey and recolor each wall-adjacent region with the opposite grey/green color\"\n}\n</annotation>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
